<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Android Auto application descriptor.

    ## `media` is deliberately NOT declared (2026-11-18)

    It used to be, with the reasoning that a MediaSession is how AA routes our
    audio to the head unit. In practice that declaration made Continuum a
    **permanently registered AA media source**: the app appeared selected in the
    head unit's app bar with no dialog present, and the car then had no audio from
    *any* app - including music - while its own native volume overlay stopped
    appearing. A head unit routed to a source that plays nothing is
    indistinguishable from a broken car.

    The owner's requirement for this work is that audio must not break other apps.
    Registering as a media source is the single most invasive thing the app does to
    a car, so it is off by default. Dialog audio then plays through the phone's own
    route rather than being presented to the head unit as a media item.

    To restore AA media playback later, add `<uses name="media" />` back - but only
    together with a session that exists strictly for the duration of a read, and
    re-verify that the car keeps audio for other apps afterwards.

    ## `notification` is kept

    MessagingStyle notifications are how dialogs appear on the head unit as
    messages, which is the intended integration: read the prompt, answer it, and
    hand the audio channel to nobody.
-->
<automotiveApp>
    <uses name="notification" />
</automotiveApp>
